1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,736
School Eight A begins with drug language and follows pharmacokinetics,
pharmacodynamics and receptor systems. We will keep those concepts
connected to one clinical question: what should this medication change
in this patient?

2
00:00:14,736 --> 00:00:26,136
A dose that is calculated correctly can still be the wrong decision.
The indication, authority, route, formulation and patient physiology
must all support giving it.

3
00:00:26,136 --> 00:00:40,848
Nancy chapter Seven provides the principal-textbook topic connection.
The school module supplies this weekend's domains; current operational
sources resolve drug and scope details rather than being silently
mixed with classroom examples.

4
00:00:40,848 --> 00:00:53,520
Scientific examples here do not authorize every listed drug or route
for a student or a PCP. Learn the mechanism, then separately verify
that you may perform the intervention in the actual setting.

5
00:00:53,520 --> 00:01:07,272
Use the generic name to identify the medication and recognize any
brand name on the package. Read the actual label every time, including
formulation, strength, route, expiry and integrity.

6
00:01:07,272 --> 00:01:19,368
A dose is the amount intended for the patient. Concentration tells you
the amount in each unit of volume. Volume equals dose divided by
concentration after their units have been aligned.

7
00:01:19,368 --> 00:01:35,376
For a fictional calculation, six milligrams from a two-milligram-per-
millilitre product requires three millilitres. If the label instead
says ten milligrams per millilitre, the same six-milligram dose
requires zero point six millilitres.

8
00:01:35,376 --> 00:01:45,792
Never identify concentration from cap colour or familiar packaging
alone. A correct medication name does not rescue a tenfold
concentration error.

9
00:01:45,792 --> 00:01:58,464
An indication is the clinical reason to use a drug. It must fit the
assessed patient, not just a symptom word. Wheeze, for example, does
not identify every underlying cause.

10
00:01:58,464 --> 00:02:12,624
A contraindication and a precaution are not identical. Read the
current source for the specific situation: one may preclude treatment,
while the other requires a changed plan, closer assessment or
consultation.

11
00:02:12,624 --> 00:02:26,856
Side effects may be expected but unwanted; an adverse effect is
harmful. A predictable effect can still be a serious adverse effect,
such as opioid respiratory depression. Expected does not mean
acceptable.

12
00:02:26,856 --> 00:02:39,648
Toxicity is not limited to deliberate overdose. An ordinary dose may
become unsafe with accumulation, organ dysfunction, interacting
medicines or unusual sensitivity.

13
00:02:39,648 --> 00:02:53,352
Pharmacokinetics asks what the body does to the drug. Absorption,
distribution, metabolism and excretion commonly overlap in time; they
are not four stages that must finish one after another.

14
00:02:53,352 --> 00:03:08,616
Intravenous administration enters systemic blood directly and bypasses
an absorption step. Calling an IV dose instantly absorbed is
convenient shorthand, but it hides the important difference from
tissue or gut delivery.

15
00:03:08,616 --> 00:03:21,816
Reaching blood is not the same as reaching the effect site or
producing peak effect. Circulation, tissue distribution and receptor
responses can still take time after an IV injection.

16
00:03:21,816 --> 00:03:33,600
After administration, compare the expected course with the observed
patient. Physiology can make a familiar drug slower, stronger or
longer acting than you anticipated.

17
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:46,584
A muscle or subcutaneous injection must reach local circulation.
Reduced tissue perfusion can delay or make absorption less
predictable; that matters when interpreting a slow response.

18
00:03:46,584 --> 00:04:00,960
The formulation also matters. A tablet must dissolve; an inhaled
aerosol must reach a useful airway location; an intranasal dose needs
an appropriate device, volume and mucosal contact.

19
00:04:00,960 --> 00:04:14,808
Surface area, solubility, ionization and transport influence membrane
passage. Do not turn a broad rule such as lipid-soluble drugs cross
easily into a claim that every tissue barrier is absolute.

20
00:04:14,808 --> 00:04:28,584
A delayed response is not permission to improvise a new route or stack
doses before the expected onset. Reassess physiology, delivery and the
indicated repeat interval while supporting immediate threats.

21
00:04:28,584 --> 00:04:42,912
Bioavailability is the fraction of a dose reaching systemic
circulation unchanged. IV delivery is the reference with one hundred
percent bioavailability; other routes depend on absorption and
presystemic loss.

22
00:04:42,912 --> 00:04:58,680
After gut absorption, portal blood passes through the liver before
systemic circulation. Metabolism in the gut wall and liver can lower
oral availability. This is first-pass metabolism, not simply a synonym
for slow absorption.

23
00:04:58,680 --> 00:05:15,288
In formal comparisons, absolute availability uses the non-IV area
under the concentration-time curve divided by the IV area, with
correction for different doses. It is not estimated just by comparing
how quickly a patient reports relief.

24
00:05:15,288 --> 00:05:29,928
Sublingual or nasal delivery can avoid much first-pass loss for the
absorbed portion, but swallowed drug and incomplete delivery still
matter. Bypassing first pass does not make different formulations
dose-equivalent.

25
00:05:29,928 --> 00:05:46,800
IV offers direct access and controlled administration, but needs an
indicated invasive procedure. IO accesses the vascular marrow space
and has specific restrictions; it is not a routine interchangeable
option for every PCP patient.

26
00:05:46,800 --> 00:06:01,008
IM and SC routes depend on tissue absorption and the product. The
school's general route times are approximate examples, not universal
onset promises or reasons to switch an indicated emergency drug
arbitrarily.

27
00:06:01,008 --> 00:06:14,640
Inhaled bronchodilator delivery often aims at airway smooth muscle
rather than requiring all medication to reach an alveolar-capillary
surface. The patient's technique and device can change the delivered
dose.

28
00:06:14,640 --> 00:06:29,520
Oral delivery can be appropriate when swallowing is safe and the
clinical goal allows its course. An unconscious or vomiting patient
may not safely receive an oral medication; speed does not outrank
aspiration risk.

29
00:06:29,520 --> 00:06:45,000
Blood carries drug to tissues. Poor cardiac output or regional
perfusion can delay effect even after direct vascular delivery. A
poorly perfused target cannot be treated as if drug arrival were
guaranteed instantly.

30
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:58,872
The apparent volume of distribution relates the amount of drug in the
body to its measured plasma concentration. Extensive tissue uptake can
produce a large calculated value, sometimes greater than actual body
water.

31
00:06:58,872 --> 00:07:11,232
A larger volume of distribution is not a claim that a new physical
tank exists. Lipid solubility, tissue binding, plasma binding and
physiological barriers all contribute.

32
00:07:11,232 --> 00:07:24,360
The blood-brain and placental barriers are selective, not complete
shields. Do not infer that a drug cannot affect the brain or fetus
merely because a simple solubility label seems reassuring.

33
00:07:24,360 --> 00:07:37,992
Many medications bind reversibly to plasma proteins such as albumin.
The unbound fraction is available to cross membranes and interact with
targets; bound drug can act as a reservoir as the balance changes.

34
00:07:37,992 --> 00:07:53,544
Low albumin or competition for binding can change the free fraction.
Clinical exposure also depends on distribution and clearance, so a
changed fraction does not mean every drug's free concentration rises
by the same fixed amount.

35
00:07:53,544 --> 00:08:06,816
Record relevant liver or kidney disease, frailty, recent drugs and the
response to previous doses. The safest adjustment is drug-specific and
guideline-based, with consultation when indicated.

36
00:08:06,816 --> 00:08:19,872
Do not copy the school's blanket instruction to always reduce every
dose in liver disease. Some drugs are contraindicated, some need
adjusted dosing and others require a different decision altogether.

37
00:08:19,872 --> 00:08:32,160
Biotransformation chemically changes a drug. The liver is important,
but metabolism is not exclusively hepatic and not every reaction
follows a strict Phase One then Phase Two sequence.

38
00:08:32,160 --> 00:08:45,360
Phase One includes oxidation, reduction and hydrolysis. Many reactions
involve cytochrome enzymes. Phase Two adds a group through
conjugation, often helping elimination.

39
00:08:45,360 --> 00:08:58,848
Metabolism does not always switch the drug off. A prodrug may need
activation, and an active metabolite may contribute to benefit or
toxicity. Phase Two products are not universally inactive.

40
00:08:58,848 --> 00:09:12,096
Reduced organ function, low perfusion and enzyme interactions may
prolong an effect or reduce activation. Use the actual medication
guidance rather than predicting one direction for every drug.

41
00:09:12,096 --> 00:09:22,560
Clearance describes an equivalent volume of plasma cleared of drug per
unit time. It is not the actual volume of urine produced, and it is
not the half-life.

42
00:09:22,560 --> 00:09:35,712
Kidneys can remove drug or metabolites through filtration and
secretion, with reabsorption influencing the net result. Other drugs
depend more on hepatic metabolism, bile or other pathways.

43
00:09:35,712 --> 00:09:48,480
If removal is reduced while doses continue, drug or active metabolite
can accumulate. A repeat dose must consider the previous doses and
elapsed time, not just the current symptom score.

44
00:09:48,480 --> 00:10:01,512
Some renal harms arise from how a drug affects kidney physiology, not
merely from poor drug clearance. An NSAID risk assessment therefore
includes renal function, perfusion and bleeding context.

45
00:10:01,512 --> 00:10:16,968
In a simplified first-order example, one half-life leaves half the
amount, two leave one quarter and four leave one sixteenth. Four or
five half-lives is substantial reduction, not mathematically complete
removal.

46
00:10:16,968 --> 00:10:29,952
If an illustrative dose is eighty units, the amounts after successive
half-lives are forty, twenty, ten and five. These units are fictional
and do not establish a redosing schedule.

47
00:10:29,952 --> 00:10:43,416
Zero-order elimination removes approximately a constant amount in a
given time over the relevant concentration range. Saturable handling
can make accumulation less predictable than a simple halving rule.

48
00:10:43,416 --> 00:10:56,400
Clinical effect may outlast a plasma level or end before complete
elimination. Active metabolites, redistribution and receptor
mechanisms make duration more than a half-life calculation.

49
00:10:56,400 --> 00:11:08,352
Onset is the time until a measurable effect begins. Peak effect is the
strongest effect for the outcome being considered. Duration is how
long that relevant effect persists.

50
00:11:08,352 --> 00:11:19,872
The highest plasma concentration is called C max. It need not occur at
the exact same moment as peak clinical effect, because effect-site
arrival and signaling can lag.

51
00:11:19,872 --> 00:11:34,248
A therapeutic window describes a useful exposure range between
inadequate effect and unacceptable toxicity. Boundaries differ between
patients and outcomes; a wide window does not make administration
error harmless.

52
00:11:34,248 --> 00:11:47,520
Before a repeat dose, ask whether the expected onset and peak have had
time to occur, whether the route worked and whether the patient needs
another intervention now. Do not wait for a medicine while ventilation
is failing.

53
00:11:47,520 --> 00:11:56,592
Pharmacodynamics asks what the drug does to the body. It includes
desired effects, adverse effects and how the effect changes with
exposure.

54
00:11:56,592 --> 00:12:09,240
Two patients may receive the same nominal dose yet have different
exposure through pharmacokinetics and different sensitivity through
pharmacodynamics. Dose alone cannot predict the entire response.

55
00:12:09,240 --> 00:12:22,584
Many drugs act at receptors, but others target enzymes, change
physical properties or participate in chemical processes. The school
COX example is an enzyme target, not a newly named receptor.

56
00:12:22,584 --> 00:12:34,056
Link the target to the treatment goal and a monitoring plan. A
mechanism explanation becomes clinically useful when it predicts both
improvement and an early warning of harm.

57
00:12:34,056 --> 00:12:46,872
Affinity describes binding tendency. Efficacy describes the ability to
generate a response in the system. Potency compares the amount or
concentration needed for a specified response.

58
00:12:46,872 --> 00:12:58,392
A drug can bind well and produce little activation, while another
produces a larger response. Binding strength alone does not determine
maximum effect or clinical suitability.

59
00:12:58,392 --> 00:13:10,680
For concentration-response curves, E C fifty is the concentration
producing half that curve's maximal response. It is not the same
statement as a dose effective in fifty percent of people.

60
00:13:10,680 --> 00:13:21,744
Compare like outcomes under comparable conditions. Potency rankings
across different routes, drugs or endpoints are not instructions to
exchange equal milligram amounts.

61
00:13:21,744 --> 00:13:34,128
On the schematic, A reaches a specified submaximal response at a lower
concentration than B. That shows greater potency for the illustrated
response, not a larger maximum.

62
00:13:34,128 --> 00:13:45,624
C reaches a lower maximum in this model. Its curve cannot be made into
A simply by moving further right. Maximal response and concentration
needed are separate features.

63
00:13:45,624 --> 00:13:57,336
Once useful response plateaus, additional drug may increase another
unwanted effect. A ceiling on one outcome does not guarantee a ceiling
on every toxicity.

64
00:13:57,336 --> 00:14:06,552
These original curves have no clinical dose scale. Practise explaining
their shapes, then use the actual drug monograph for safe patient
dosing.

65
00:14:06,552 --> 00:14:18,240
An agonist activates a receptor. A full agonist can produce the
reference maximal response in a particular system, while a partial
agonist has a lower maximal response there.

66
00:14:18,240 --> 00:14:29,952
Morphine is the school's full opioid-agonist example and buprenorphine
the partial-agonist example. These are mechanism comparisons, not
permission for routine PCP administration.

67
00:14:29,952 --> 00:14:43,728
A partial agonist may compete with a full agonist and reduce the
response. In an opioid-dependent patient, certain transitions can
precipitate withdrawal; the clinical plan must use the applicable
protocol.

68
00:14:43,728 --> 00:14:56,112
Do not infer that a partial agonist cannot cause serious respiratory
depression, especially with other depressants. The school's ceiling-
effect language is not a guarantee of harmless overdose.

69
00:14:56,112 --> 00:15:09,912
A competitive antagonist competes with an agonist for binding at the
receptor. In an ideal experimental model, enough agonist can overcome
the blockade; this is not permission to give an unsafe clinical dose.

70
00:15:09,912 --> 00:15:22,656
Noncompetitive mechanisms reduce response in a way that additional
agonist cannot simply reverse. Describe the actual target rather than
using antagonist as a loose name for every inhibitor.

71
00:15:22,656 --> 00:15:36,816
Naloxone blocks opioid receptor effects. Reversal can restore
ventilation, but the opioid may last longer and non-opioid causes may
coexist. Continue airway support and reassessment.

72
00:15:36,816 --> 00:15:55,656
Organophosphates inhibit acetylcholinesterase, allowing acetylcholine
to accumulate. The school calls them an irreversible agonist in one
passage; that is not a direct receptor mechanism. Atropine blocks
muscarinic effects but does not repair every toxic effect.

73
00:15:55,656 --> 00:16:09,336
Opioid receptor activation can reduce pain, but also lower respiratory
drive and awareness. Miosis, nausea, reduced gut activity and
circulatory effects may accompany the response.

74
00:16:09,336 --> 00:16:22,200
Assess respiratory rate together with depth, air movement, airway
protection and mental status. A displayed saturation on oxygen can
remain reassuring while ventilation worsens.

75
00:16:22,200 --> 00:16:35,040
An opioid toxidrome is a pattern, not a requirement that every sign be
present. Do not withhold ventilation because pupils are not pinpoint
or because the exact substance is unknown.

76
00:16:35,040 --> 00:16:48,984
Naloxone may improve opioid-related ventilation, but recurrence and
withdrawal are possible. Use the actual dosing pathway and continue
reassessment rather than declaring the patient cured after awakening.

77
00:16:48,984 --> 00:17:02,328
Alpha one activation generally increases vascular tone. Beta one
effects include increased heart rate, contractility and conduction.
Beta two activation relaxes bronchial smooth muscle.

78
00:17:02,328 --> 00:17:16,608
Epinephrine acts across alpha and beta systems. That helps explain its
role in anaphylaxis, where airway, breathing and circulation can all
be threatened. Route and dose remain indication-specific.

79
00:17:16,608 --> 00:17:29,136
Salbutamol preferentially targets beta two receptors, but selectivity
is not absolute. Reassess airflow and work of breathing as well as
tremor, heart rate and other adverse effects.

80
00:17:29,136 --> 00:17:41,904
A faster pulse after a bronchodilator does not by itself prove
treatment failure or success. Compare breathing, perfusion, patient
experience and the time course before deciding what to do next.

81
00:17:41,904 --> 00:17:55,104
Muscarinic receptor activity can increase secretions, constrict
bronchi, slow the heart and increase gastrointestinal activity.
Effects depend on the receptor location and physiological context.

82
00:17:55,104 --> 00:18:10,920
The school mnemonic SLUDGEM lists salivation, lacrimation, urination,
defecation, gastrointestinal upset, emesis and miosis. Its three Bs
highlight bronchorrhea, bronchospasm and bradycardia.

83
00:18:10,920 --> 00:18:25,176
Atropine and ipratropium block muscarinic actions in different
indications. A cholinergic poisoning can also affect nicotinic and
central pathways, so atropine is not a complete cure for every
manifestation.

84
00:18:25,176 --> 00:18:40,176
Do not enter a contaminated area to administer an antidote without the
required protection. Scene safety, appropriate decontamination,
ventilation and specialist support are part of the treatment, not
optional extras.

85
00:18:40,176 --> 00:18:55,824
H one signaling contributes to itch, vascular changes and allergic
manifestations. H two signaling includes gastric acid secretion. The
shared word histamine does not make the receptors or treatments
interchangeable.

86
00:18:55,824 --> 00:19:09,672
First-generation H one medicines may cause sedation and
anticholinergic effects. Dimenhydrinate and diphenhydramine share some
pharmacology, but their indications and authorization are not
identical.

87
00:19:09,672 --> 00:19:24,024
Life-threatening anaphylaxis needs epinephrine and airway, breathing
and circulation care. Relief of itch does not correct shock or
dangerous airway swelling, and an antihistamine must not delay
epinephrine.

88
00:19:24,024 --> 00:19:37,272
Do not decide that a reaction is harmless merely because a non-IgE
mechanism is suspected. Judge physiological compromise; immune and
non-immune mechanisms can produce serious systemic reactions.

89
00:19:37,272 --> 00:19:49,848
The school uses the serotonin receptor five H T three to connect gut
and central emesis pathways. Ondansetron blocks that receptor and can
reduce nausea and vomiting.

90
00:19:49,848 --> 00:20:02,112
This is different from the vestibular and anticholinergic effects
emphasized for dimenhydrinate. Similar symptom goals do not imply
identical mechanisms, doses or eligibility.

91
00:20:02,112 --> 00:20:17,808
Before treating the symptom, assess its context: onset, pain,
neurological findings, perfusion, pregnancy possibility, ingestion and
relevant history. Nausea may accompany an important underlying
emergency.

92
00:20:17,808 --> 00:20:30,360
After an authorized antiemetic, reassess symptom response, safety and
the underlying problem. A patient who stops vomiting has not
necessarily become safe to leave or delay.

93
00:20:30,360 --> 00:20:42,072
Cyclooxygenase enzymes support prostaglandin and thromboxane pathways.
These affect pain, inflammation, fever, vascular physiology and
platelet activity.

94
00:20:42,072 --> 00:20:54,192
ASA irreversibly inhibits platelet cyclooxygenase and reduces
thromboxane-mediated aggregation for the affected platelet's lifespan.
It does not directly dissolve an existing clot.

95
00:20:54,192 --> 00:21:07,152
Ibuprofen and ketorolac are other COX-inhibiting medicines with
analgesic benefits and renal, gastrointestinal and bleeding risks.
They cannot simply substitute for ASA's acute-coronary role.

96
00:21:07,152 --> 00:21:21,336
The school briefly describes ASA as binding platelet receptors. The
more precise target here is an enzyme. Match the actual mechanism to
the indication, and check the specific contraindications.

97
00:21:21,336 --> 00:21:37,032
An adult has reduced awareness and inadequate slow breathing. A
bystander reports an unknown tablet. Start the immediate assessment
and support airway and ventilation; do not wait to identify the
receptor before treating failure.

98
00:21:37,032 --> 00:21:49,056
If an opioid contribution is suspected and naloxone is indicated and
authorized, use the actual pathway. Name the goal as improved
functional ventilation, then monitor the result.

99
00:21:49,056 --> 00:22:01,176
No immediate change can reflect delivery, onset, dose or a different
or mixed cause. Automatic repeated doses without those checks can
waste time and create harm.

100
00:22:01,176 --> 00:22:14,544
An initial improvement followed by decline can reflect a longer-acting
opioid or another problem. PK predicts recurrence risk, PD explains
the reversal, and assessment decides the next safe action.

101
00:22:14,544 --> 00:22:26,784
Without notes, distinguish dose from concentration and
pharmacokinetics from pharmacodynamics. Explain why immediate blood
entry does not guarantee immediate peak effect.

102
00:22:26,784 --> 00:22:36,960
Sketch the half-life sequence and compare potency with maximal
response. Then explain why the sketch cannot tell you an actual
patient's safe dose.

103
00:22:36,960 --> 00:22:49,992
For opioid, adrenergic, muscarinic, histamine, serotonin and COX
targets, give an intended effect and one important harm or limitation.
Say what you would reassess.

104
00:22:49,992 --> 00:23:03,552
Next, apply these mechanisms to the school's medication-safety, adult
monograph, pediatric calculation and scope sections. Correct science
is necessary, but it is not the complete authorization decision.
